<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Great Mosque of Manisa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1366–<a href="/years/1367">1367</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manisa">Manisa</a> is a mosque in Manisa, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, built in 1366–1367 by Ishak Bey, ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> of Saruhan. Standing on the slope of Mount Sipylus, it combines a domed prayer hall with an arcaded courtyard and is joined by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> and the founder's tomb. It is one of the most important monuments of the Anatolian beyliks before the Ottoman period.</p></div>
